course: dictionary
lesson: 11182
title: Wireless
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are built from smaller parts that help you work out their meaning. Wireless is made of wire and -less. A wire is a thin strand of metal that carries electricity, and -less means without. So wireless means without wires. «Wifi is a wireless link between a device and a nearby router» tells you the link works without a cable.
explain: Wireless is mostly an adjective, a describing word, as in wireless headphones or a wireless network. «A wireless charger has a coil in the pad» tells you the phone sits on a pad and charges with no cable plugged into it. Ask yourself: does the word describe a thing, and does the context say it has no wires?
explain: Wireless can also be a noun. In old-fashioned British English, a wireless is a radio set. Families in the 1930s and 1940s gathered round the wireless to hear the news during the war. If you meet the word in a history book, look at the clues: news, listening and families point to a radio.
fact: Wireless means working without wires.
fact: Wireless devices send information through the air using radio waves.
fact: Wi-Fi, wireless headphones and wireless charging are all everyday examples.
fact: In old-fashioned British English, a wireless is a radio set.
fact: The -less at the end of a word means without, like hopeless or careless.
exercise: «A wireless charger has a coil in the pad that makes a changing magnetic field.» What does wireless mean in this sentence? | *Working without wires | Very fast and noisy | Turned off | Made of very thin metal
exercise: «The internet is the worldwide network that joins computers; wifi is a wireless link between a device and a nearby router.» Find and copy the word that describes a link with no cable. | computers | network | *wireless | worldwide
exercise: Grandma says, «We listened to the wireless every night during the war.» What is a wireless? | *A radio set | A thin metal wire | A map of the sea | A computer game
test: Ella's headphones connect to her tablet with no cable at all. Which word describes them? | Loud | Round | Heavy | *Wireless
test: The word wireless is made of two parts: wire and -less. What does -less mean? | Again | Very | *Without | Full of
exam: Mum says, «Wireless charging is so quick.» Which of these is a wireless thing? | A cable | A plug in the wall | A wired mouse | *A wireless charger
